[2026-01-14T01:17:24.734000] I have analyzed the Smith-Waterman algorithm's Go implementation. The core `ConstructMatrix` function has a wavefront dependency, making simple parallelization incorrect. I will use an anti-diagonal approach, which is a correct way to parallelize this type of problem. The `FindHighestScore` function is a simple reduction that can be parallelized by splitting the matrix into row chunks. The `Traceback` is inherently sequential. I'll use a bounded worker pool based on `runtime.NumCPU()` and include a sequential fallback for small matrices to manage resources and overhead. Determinism will be ensured by the fixed computation order of the anti-diagonal strategy and a fixed-order reduction for finding the highest score. I have already saved the request and the sequential baseline. Next, I will implement the parallel version.
